# Wallet UI

`WalletApp` owns the wait-times query, both stores, the open card, the tab, and toasts. The tab and open card are the URL: `/` is Puentes, `/explore` is Explorar, `/schedules` is Alarmas, and `/{portNumber}` is that card open. The tab bar has four tabs and only the active one shows its name. Panes receive that snapshot and callbacks. They do not write storage. A crossing loads its own hourly average when it is on screen.

The `["wait-times"]` query starts from the server snapshot, treats it as fresh for 30 seconds, and refetches every 60 seconds. `loading` means `isPending && !data`, so a failed first load can show the error state.

Below 1024px, `.pane[data-active]` shows one tab and `TabBar` switches them. The shell is fixed to the viewport and the active pane scrolls inside it, so the document never scrolls and the tab bar stays absolute at the bottom of that shell. That scroller is `overflow-x: clip` (not `hidden`) so a vertical drag still scrolls; with `hidden`, Safari selects the control under the finger instead. At `lg` and up the tab bar is hidden, both panes stay on the page, and the wallet column scrolls inside its own sticky pane.

`WalletPane` renders `CardStack` (`CardFace` and `CardDetail`). `expandedId` is the `/{portNumber}` route. Closing the card, Escape, or switching to Explorar goes back to `/` or `/explore`. If that port leaves the feed, the card closes.

A short tap on a closed card opens it. Holding one still for about half a second picks it up; the card then tracks the finger, fades to 82% opacity so the drop target underneath stays visible, and the others spring into the gap. `CardStack` reports the drop through `onReorder` and does not write storage. Reorder is off while a card is open, and with a single card. Alt+ArrowUp and Alt+ArrowDown move the focused card for the keyboard.

Swiping the front card — the last one, fully visible — left or right sends it to the back of the stack. It tracks the finger. Past a short distance, or on a flick, it leaves that way and settles into the back. A short swipe springs back. The same move is off while a card is open, and with a single card. A horizontal move on a card behind the front still cancels the hold; a vertical move still scrolls.

Dragging the open card down closes it. That close starts where the finger let go and settles straight into the card's slot. A short drag springs back to the open card.

A schedule notification opens with `routes` and `lane` on the query. `WalletPane` then shows those bridges as full cards, one above the other (`RouteRow`), instead of the peeking stack, until Done or Escape. A known `lane` is used when that crossing offers it. An unknown lane still shows the bridges, each on its usual lane. A tap still opens a card; closing it returns to the cards. The query stays across tabs. Dismissing drops the query and the stack returns in place, without the fan-in.

A card that can be reordered keeps `touch-action: none` from the very first touch, not just once the hold completes. `touch-action` is decided once at the start of a touch gesture and ignores later changes, so turning it off only after the hold would be too late — the browser would already be panning the page. Because `touch-action` is off the whole time, an early move that cancels the hold (over `SLOP` before `HOLD_MS`) does its own scroll of `document.scrollingElement` by hand, so swiping past a card still scrolls the list normally.

`BrowsePane` searches and filters the snapshot already in memory (`mexican`, `canadian`, or `all`). Sorting uses a fixed `en-US` collator so the server and client agree.

Motion lives in `motion.ts`. Use `SPRING.move` for layout, `snap` for small state changes, `release` after a flick, and `pop` for badges. When `useReducedMotion()` is set, use `REDUCED` or skip the travel. Card height is `CARD_H` (212) and the visible strip is `PEEK` (76). Stack height comes from `stackHeight`.

A tap on refresh flips the wait digits on the cards while that check runs, then they settle. The minute refetch does not flip them. Reduced motion pulses the number instead of moving it.

The live wait is minutes until it reaches an hour, then hours and minutes (`1 hr 30 min`). `WaitDuration` draws it. The usual wait for this hour sits under the live number on `CardFace`, so the front card and an open card show it. Stacked cards cover that line. `CardDetail` and an expanded browse row show it on every published lane. Under that, `UsualDay` draws the same hours as a bar chart for the lane on screen: the quietest stretch, and a scrub that reads one hour, including the highest wait CBP lists for it. It is the CBP hourly average for that lane, not a blend of the lanes on screen, and not today's partial wait. `useLaneAverages` loads it for a crossing that is on screen. A lane with no average leaves the line out, and the chart says so.

Nuevo (Gateway) and Viejo (B&M) show a live camera at the top of `CardDetail` and of an expanded browse row. `crossingStream` in `lib/ports.ts` is the only map: a fallback playlist plus the IPCamLive alias. The playlist everyone plays comes from `camera_stream`, through `getCameraStreams()` (`"use cache"`, tag `camera-streams`, `cacheLife("days")`). `WalletApp` starts from that snapshot and refetches `GET /api/camera-streams` about once a minute. The player mounts when that view opens and unmounts when it closes, so a stacked card does not keep a stream open. If loading the playlist fails, the player `POST`s the alias and that address. The server checks it, because the 404 page has no CORS header and the browser cannot read the status. A 404 looks the alias up, upserts `camera_stream`, and `revalidateTag`s `camera-streams` with `expire: 0`. Any other result still shows the camera as unavailable.

Other crossings with public cameras show `CrossingFeeds` in the same place, and only when `crossingStream` has nothing. `feedsForPort(portNumber)` in `lib/camera-feeds.ts` is the only map from a CBP port to its cameras (Nogales, Laredo, the Ambassador Bridge, Blue Water, Peace Arch, Sumas). Each camera is an HLS stream, a snapshot, or an embedded player, and has a stable `id`. Chips under the picture switch views, and so does dragging the picture sideways (`useSwipe`: 1:1 tracking, rubber band at the ends, momentum projection, interruptible settle; plain slide with reduced motion). The settle and the hand-off to the next view are the same `SPRING.release` the wallet cards swipe with, carrying the finger's own velocity, so it reads as the same gesture as the stack, not a separate, stiffer one. That drag ticks once it has travelled far enough to switch on release, ticks again if it comes back out of that zone, and ticks softer when it bumps the resisted edge; a flick that commits without crossing the line ticks once at release instead. A chip tap still ticks on its own. Only one view is mounted at a time. Every camera shows a spinner, a soft shimmer and "Connecting…" until its first frame. Every camera, Nuevo and Viejo included, ends in the shared `CameraBar` (`camera-bar.tsx`): it sits on the card surface and follows the theme, and Nuevo and Viejo read their source (Comtodo) from `CROSSING_STREAM` in `lib/ports.ts`. The bar reads "Updated 2 min ago" for a snapshot and "Live" for video, and the i button opens the provider, whether it is official. The provider name is the link to the source page. The address everyone plays comes from `camera_feed` through `getCameraFeeds()` (`"use cache"`, tag `camera-feeds`), seeded from the file on first read, and `GET /api/camera-feeds` is the client copy. A failed playlist is checked once through `POST /api/camera-feeds`; only a confirmed 404 with a lookup (Nogales) saves a new address. Snapshots never go straight to the browser. `/api/camera-snapshot/{id}` shares one fetch per camera every 45 seconds, reads age from `Last-Modified` or from a changed hash, saves it in `camera_snapshot`, and marks the camera live, stale (no new picture for 10 minutes), or down. The picture URL carries the hash, so it reloads only on a change. HLS goes straight to the host in Safari, and through `/api/camera-proxy` everywhere else or after a failure. The proxy serves only the hosts in `PROXY_HOSTS`.

Share sits on the bottom right of the card, so the front card and an open card can send the live wait for the lane on screen. A clock button sits just left of it and opens the wait alert for that lane. Either press does not open, reorder, or swipe the card. The share sheet is used when the browser has one. Otherwise that same message is copied and a toast says so. The link is `/{portNumber}?lane=&lang=&from=`. `from` is the account id of the person who shared it. Notification links omit it. Opening it selects that lane. The page title, description, and preview image use that same wait, and the preview URL keeps `from` when the link had one. A visit that is not signed in stores `from` in the `usbordertime.from` cookie for 30 days and drops it from the address bar. The first link wins until sign-in. `ensureProfile` writes `profile.referred_by` only when that profile is created on the visit, and only when the id already belongs to a profile. An account that already existed is left as it was. `InviteClaim`, inside `SessionGate`, clears the cookie once this browser has a session.

A phone stays portrait: the manifest `orientation` is `portrait`, and `PortraitLock` asks `screen.orientation` for the same where the browser allows it. Expanding the camera — the video's fullscreen presentation, including iPhone's native player — releases that lock so the picture can turn. Closing it locks portrait again. A desktop window is left free.

Haptics are `haptic()` in `lib/haptics.ts`. A control ticks only when its value changes. Tabs, lanes, the border filter, the language switch, and the theme switch are selection ticks. Saving a crossing or pinning a city confirms. Removing a card is heavier. Sharing ticks on press, before the sheet opens. Refresh ticks on press and again when the digits settle. The minute refetch stays quiet. Picking a card up, crossing a reorder slot, setting it down, rubbing past the ends of the stack, dragging an open card past the dismiss line, swiping the front card past the send-back line, and dragging between camera views past the commit line each tick with the gesture. A flick that sends the card back without having crossed that line ticks when it commits. On iPhone a tap lands on `HapticPad`, a transparent switch, because that is the tick Safari still plays. A drag that starts on the switch still scrolls: the switch does not take the gesture, and the control under it does not fire. If Safari will not pan, the pad moves the scroller itself. Drags call `haptic()` directly. Reduced motion does not mute haptics.

A tap that switches tabs plays that tab's icon animation. Reduced motion skips it.

On a phone the header stays mounted across tabs. The title and subtitle crossfade, and a header button that appears or leaves pops. The streak and the account button stay still. The pane body slides in from the side you moved toward. The header's fill and blur stay off until that scroller leaves the top, so the color behind the title shows through before you scroll. Once content passes underneath, that layer eases in, and it eases back out on the way to the top.

`AccountButton` opens the name, the language and appearance switches, and sign out. When this Google account is listed in the `ADMIN_EMAILS` env var, the menu also links to `/admin`. On the crossings list it is hidden at `lg`, because the bridges header stays on screen. Sign-out returns to the sign-in screen. The account keeps the bridges; this browser keeps a copy either way. Sign-in, privacy, and the missing page keep the round switches, because those screens have no profile menu.

A new account answers how they found Border Time before the bridges. The choices live in `lib/referral.ts`. The pick is saved on `profile.referral` through `POST /api/referral`, and the question does not return. Accounts that already existed are not asked. Signing out from that screen leaves the question for the next sign-in.

Right after that, a new account is asked whether to find their closest bridges from their location or pick them by hand (`LocationPrompt`, gated on `profile.location_pending`). Sharing location calls the browser's geolocation, then `nearbyBridges()` in `lib/port-locations.ts` — a static lat/lng per published city, matched by haversine distance — picks every city within 40 miles of it, not only the single nearest one (El Paso's Ysleta and Santa Teresa, the Rio Grande Valley's Hidalgo and Progreso, are all "nearby" together), always keeping at least the nearest city even past that radius. Those crossings are added to `walletStore` and every matched city is pinned in `pinStore`, the same two client stores Browse writes to, so `PreferenceSync` uploads them once the wallet mounts. The result pauses on a "found" screen first — a check mark, the matched city or cities (`Intl.ListFormat` joins more than one), and each bridge staggering in with its own check, its city shown only when more than one city matched — so what got added is actually seen, not swapped in behind a silent refresh. Continue is what moves on from there. Picking manually adds nothing; the wallet opens empty and Browse (`BrowsePane`) is how they add bridges by hand. Either choice clears `location_pending` through `POST /api/location-onboarding` and the question does not return. A denied or failed geolocation lookup, or a feed with no city `nearbyBridges` can place, leaves the question up with an inline message instead of silently falling back. Signing out from that screen leaves the question for the next sign-in, same as the referral prompt.

In development, the account menu has "Replay location prompt" (`DevLocationReplay`), which `DELETE /api/location-onboarding` (404 outside development) uses to set `location_pending` back to true for this account so the flow can be run again without a new sign-up. `/location-lab` (404 outside development, not linked) drives the real `LocationPrompt` with a stubbed `getCurrentPosition` and a Brownsville-plus-Progreso fixture feed, so the ask → locating → found animation can be watched with no Google sign-in and no device location prompt — see `components/wallet/location-lab.tsx`.

Below `lg`, sign-in is a translucent card over the bottom of the sign-in screen: "Sign in with Google" and "Swipe up", with light spilling from its top edge and two chevrons pointing up. The card is the swipe area and tracks the finger. Passing the commit line ticks `medium`. Letting go ticks `success` and starts Google sign-in when the release projects past that line, or ticks `soft` and the card springs home when it does not. The page behind stays put. A tap on the card's button still signs in. At `lg` and up the card is hidden and the Google button on the page remains. While Google takes the page, a veil covers the screen with a spinner and the signing-in line. Reduced motion keeps the light and chevrons still, and pulses the spinner instead of turning it.

`WalletPane` shows `InstallBanner` under the header while no card is open. The wait alert itself is the clock button on the card, described in `alerts.mdc`.

The empty wallet suggests unsaved Mexican crossings whose city is Brownsville.
